\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Harmonic cochains realize the Betti numbers}
\date{2026-11-02}

\p{Let #{C^0,\ldots,C^n} be finite-dimensional real vector spaces and
let #{\extd_k:C^k\to C^{k+1}} be linear maps with
#{\extd_{k+1}\circ \extd_k=0}. Degree
#{0} has no incoming map, and degree #{n} has no outgoing map. Each
degree has a positive-definite inner product. Its adjoint is the
unique linear map obeying
#{\langle \extd_k\alpha,\beta\rangle_{k+1}
=\langle\alpha,\extd_k^\dagger\beta\rangle_k}.
No Gram matrix enters this definition.}

\p{The Hodge Laplacian of degree #{k} is
#{\Delta_k=\extd_{k-1}\extd_{k-1}^\dagger+\extd_k^\dagger\extd_k},
with either summand omitted at an endpoint. A cochain #{\gamma} is
harmonic when #{\Delta_k\gamma=0}. Then
#{\ker\Delta_k} is isomorphic to
#{H^k=\ker \extd_k/\operatorname{im}\extd_{k-1}}, and
#{C^k} is the orthogonal sum
#{\operatorname{im}\extd_{k-1}\oplus\ker\Delta_k\oplus\operatorname{im}\extd_k^\dagger}.
The kernel of #{\extd+\extdag} has dimension equal to the sum of the Betti
numbers.}

\p{On the square torus of \ref{fdip-008Z}, the evaluation pairing
meets the hypotheses. The edge adjoint is the
\vocabk{counting adjoint}{fdip-0007}, and at edge weight #{1} the
vertex operator is the negative of the unit-weight
\vocabk{weighted graph Laplacian}{fdip-0007}. The Betti numbers
#{(1,2,1)} give kernel dimension #{4}. The constant vertex
functions, the two winding edge fields, and the constant face
function form a basis of counting-harmonic cochains. A vertex
function lies in the counting-adjoint image exactly when its values
sum to zero. A different positive-definite edge product can leave a
winding field with nonzero adjoint.}

\proof{
\p{Nondegeneracy gives a unique adjoint. Declare a basis of each
degree and let #{G_k} be the Gram matrix of its inner product. Both
sides of
#{\extd_k^\dagger=G_k^{-1}\extd_k^{T}G_{k+1}}
equal #{\alpha^{T}\extd_k^{T}G_{k+1}\beta}, since #{G_k} cancels
on the right. Identity Gram matrices make this adjoint the counting
transpose. Adjoints reverse composition, so
#{\extd_k^\dagger\circ \extd_{k+1}^\dagger=0}. On one degree,
#{(\extd+\extdag)\circ(\extd+\extdag)=\Delta_k} because #{\extd\circ\extd} and
#{\extdag\circ\extdag} vanish, and
##{\langle\Delta_k\gamma,\gamma\rangle
=\langle \extd_k\gamma,\extd_k\gamma\rangle
+\langle \extd_{k-1}^\dagger\gamma,\extd_{k-1}^\dagger\gamma\rangle.}
Thus #{\Delta_k\gamma=0} exactly when both maps vanish on #{\gamma}.
On the orthogonal sum of the degrees, #{\extd+\extdag} vanishes exactly on
harmonic tuples, so the kernel dimension adds.}

\p{Equal ranks give
#{\dim\ker \extd_{k-1}^\dagger=\dim C^k-\operatorname{rank}\extd_{k-1}}.
If #{\extd_{k-1}\alpha} lies in that kernel, then
#{\langle \extd_{k-1}\alpha,\extd_{k-1}\alpha\rangle
=\langle\alpha,\extd_{k-1}^\dagger\extd_{k-1}\alpha\rangle=0},
so #{\extd_{k-1}\alpha=0}. The sum of image and kernel is therefore
all of #{C^k}. A closed cochain splits as #{\extd_{k-1}\alpha+\rho}
with #{\extd_{k-1}^\dagger\rho=0} and #{\extd_k\rho=0}, hence #{\rho} is
harmonic. Harmonic coboundaries vanish, so the class map is an
isomorphism onto #{H^k}. The same count splits off
#{\operatorname{im}\extd_k^\dagger}. Cross pairings among the three summands
vanish by that adjoint identity.}

\p{The torus Gram matrices are identities. Constants are closed and
have no incoming adjoint. Each winding is closed, and the
[counting adjoint](fdip-0007) returns zero, since one edge enters
each vertex and one leaves it with equal values. The constant face function is closed. Summing a coboundary over
every face cancels (\ref{fdip-008Z}), so the adjoint vanishes. The 4 cochains occupy three
degrees, and the two edge fields are not scalar multiples, so they
are independent. Kernel dimension #{4} makes them a basis. The
pairing with the constant #{1} is the vertex sum. When the horizontal side has length at least #{2}, one horizontal
edge of weight #{2} and all other edges of weight #{1} leave that
winding closed, and the adjoint at its head equals #{1}. On a horizontal loop the
two contributions agree.}
}

\p{Remark. Once a basis of each degree has been declared, the Gram matrices
#{G_k} of the inner products satisfy
#{\extd_k^\dagger=G_k^{-1}\extd_k^{T}G_{k+1}}. Identity Gram matrices make this
adjoint the counting transpose. The proof above derives that
formula, and the Hodge decomposition itself does not use it.}
